# Single source of truth for every Talos and Kubernetes version pin the
# automated upgrade mechanism uses.
#
# This file feeds a `configMapGenerator` in kustomization.yaml, which both
# deploys it as the `talos-versions` ConfigMap (inspect with `kubectl -n
# cattle-system get cm talos-versions -o yaml`) and, via the `replacements`
# block in the same file, projects these three values into every Plan in
# plans/ and into the fleet-health CronJob. A version bump means editing the
# three lines below -- nothing else in this directory.
#
# ── The one pin this file cannot reach ───────────────────────────────────────
# cluster/overlays/*/talos-machineconfigs/controlplane.yaml's `install.image`
# is Talos machine config, not a Kubernetes resource -- it is applied by hand
# via `talosctl apply-config`/`patch`, entirely outside Kustomize and Flux, so
# Kustomize has no way to reach into it. Renovate still edits it directly
# (see renovate.json), and the Talos image CI workflow verifies it agrees with
# TALOS_VERSION/SCHEMATIC_ID below on every PR that touches either file. That
# makes this repo's version pins two independently-edited sites instead of
# one, but both are enforced to agree, and down from the nine it used to be.
#
# ── TALOS_VERSION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Renovate watches this (regexManager in renovate.json at the repo root) and
# is never auto-merged: the upgrade reboots nodes, and the Image Factory
# builds per (schematic, version) pair, so a release can land before an image
# exists for these extensions. The Talos image CI workflow checks both.
TALOS_VERSION=v1.13.0

# ── KUBERNETES_VERSION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Kubernetes control-plane/kubelet version, upgraded independently of
# TALOS_VERSION via `talosctl upgrade-k8s` (see plans/talos-kubernetes-upgrade.yaml).
# Talos bundles a default Kubernetes version per release, but upgrade-k8s lets
# this move on its own schedule -- tracked here rather than left implicit so a
# Talos bump can never silently drag the Kubernetes version with it. Renovate
# watches this too, grouped separately from `talos` and never auto-merged for
# the same reboot/compatibility reasons. The Talos image CI workflow confirms
# this resolves to a real published Kubernetes release, and (separately) that
# a change to this value advances at most one minor -- talosctl upgrade-k8s
# only supports an N -> N or N -> N+1 step (upgrade.Path.IsSupported()
# upstream), and Renovate's regex manager has no way to propose anything
# other than the newest release, so that has to be enforced in CI rather than
# left to what Renovate happens to suggest.
#
# Set to v1.36.3, Talos v1.13.9's own DefaultKubernetesVersion (confirmed
# against pkg/machinery/constants/constants.go at the v1.13.9 tag) -- not a
# guess. Picking anything else here for the FIRST value this repo ever pins
# risks the exact failure the two checks above exist to catch: this cluster
# has never run upgrade-k8s, so its live version is presumably still
# whatever Talos 1.13.9 bundled, and pinning something more than one minor
# away would make the very first automated run fail after SUC has already
# cordoned the node. Once this has run for real, bump it one minor at a time.
KUBERNETES_VERSION=v1.36.4

# ── SCHEMATIC_ID ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Derived from schematic.yaml, not chosen -- a content hash of the extension
# list, independent of TALOS_VERSION. Only regenerate it when schematic.yaml
# itself changes:
#   curl -X POST --data-binary @schematic.yaml https://factory.talos.dev/schematics
# Extensions baked in: siderolabs/iscsi-tools, siderolabs/util-linux-tools --
# both required by Longhorn V1, see docs/adr/0004-longhorn-v1-storage-engine.md
SCHEMATIC_ID=613e1592b2da41ae5e265e8789429f22e121aab91cb4deb6bc3c0b6262961245

# ── Rollback ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reverting a version bump in this file and letting it sync is the rollback
# mechanism: system-upgrade-controller reconciles toward whatever spec.version
# says regardless of whether that is higher or lower than what is running, so
# a revert re-triggers the exact same reviewed, windowed/on-demand upgrade
# path in reverse. That is a convenience, not a guarantee -- Talos does not
# uniformly support downgrading across arbitrary version pairs, and a
# Kubernetes apiserver can refuse to serve objects a newer version already
# wrote in a newer schema. The CI workflow requires a `confirmed-downgrade`
# label on any PR that decreases either version above, so a revert gets the
# same scrutiny a forward bump does rather than applying unconditionally.
